1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,410
Jonathon: For two years I took 1604 home at
six-fifteen, on purpose, to miss bath time.

2
00:00:06,820 --> 00:00:09,890
Wes Hart: 1604 at
six-fifteen is a parking lot.

3
00:00:10,082 --> 00:00:11,470
Jonathon: I picked the parking lot.

4
00:00:11,610 --> 00:00:15,732
Then one night my wife goes,
"Since when do you like 1604?"

5
00:00:18,382 --> 00:00:21,350
Wes Hart: This is Walk
Steadfast. I'm Wes Hart.

6
00:00:21,710 --> 00:00:24,687
I've sat at tables with
men for twenty-some years.

7
00:00:25,007 --> 00:00:25,930
Jonathon: I'm Jonathon.

8
00:00:26,130 --> 00:00:29,247
I came to faith at twenty-nine,
the year I met Wes.

9
00:00:29,437 --> 00:00:35,050
Wes Hart: Starting today, twice a week, we
talk straight about following Jesus as a man.

10
00:00:35,431 --> 00:00:39,450
Jonathon: Wes wrote a book called Steadfast
Men, and a video training goes with it.

11
00:00:39,670 --> 00:00:41,931
Both are at walksteadfast.com.

12
00:00:42,285 --> 00:00:46,361
Wes Hart: Today we ask why good
intentions don't make it to Tuesday.

13
00:00:46,853 --> 00:00:47,903
Jonathon: Episode one.

14
00:00:48,213 --> 00:00:50,094
Jonathon: The Man You Keep Saying You'll Be.

15
00:00:51,156 --> 00:00:52,588
Wes Hart: Pull up a chair.

16
00:00:53,029 --> 00:00:54,250
Jonathon: Sorry. Sorry.

17
00:00:54,410 --> 00:00:58,210
My daughter just texted me "good
luck daddy" and a picture of a dog.

18
00:00:58,539 --> 00:00:59,788
Wes Hart: You don't have a dog.

19
00:01:00,170 --> 00:01:03,326
Jonathon: We don't have a dog.
I think it's a negotiation.

20
00:01:03,876 --> 00:01:05,180
Wes Hart: Well. Welcome.

21
00:01:05,190 --> 00:01:08,960
This is the first one, so I want to tell
you what's going to happen today, because

22
00:01:08,970 --> 00:01:11,943
it's a little longer than
what we'll normally do.

23
00:01:12,269 --> 00:01:12,909
Jonathon: A little.

24
00:01:13,400 --> 00:01:17,510
Wes Hart: A little. Three
things. First, who we are.

25
00:01:17,890 --> 00:01:19,540
You should know who you're sitting with.

26
00:01:19,550 --> 00:01:25,600
Then we're going to actually do the thing.
A real conversation. About Tuesdays.

27
00:01:25,610 --> 00:01:31,540
And before we let you go, what this whole
thing is, the book, the training, the

28
00:01:31,550 --> 00:01:36,230
website, all of it, laid out once
so you never have to wonder.

29
00:01:36,550 --> 00:01:39,032
Jonathon: About one Tuesday in
particular, which is today.

30
00:01:39,521 --> 00:01:40,370
Wes Hart: Which is today.

31
00:01:40,770 --> 00:01:44,122
And the question underneath
all of it is pretty simple.

32
00:01:44,471 --> 00:01:49,130
Wes Hart: What kind of man are you
on a regular day? Not your best day.

33
00:01:49,570 --> 00:01:52,961
Not the day with the pictures. A Tuesday.

34
00:01:53,389 --> 00:01:57,490
Jonathon: And I'll tell you right now, my answer
to that question was bad for a long time.

35
00:01:57,706 --> 00:01:58,620
Wes Hart: Mine too.

36
00:01:59,093 --> 00:02:03,010
Wes Hart: And if you're twenty-two and
nobody's waiting on you at home, stay.

37
00:02:03,530 --> 00:02:08,130
Your Tuesday's in here too.
We'll get to you before the half.

38
00:02:08,410 --> 00:02:09,270
Jonathon: We'll get there.

39
00:02:09,470 --> 00:02:13,340
But first, people need to know who you
are, and you're terrible at talking about

40
00:02:13,350 --> 00:02:14,061
yourself.

41
00:02:14,340 --> 00:02:15,265
Wes Hart: I am.

42
00:02:15,600 --> 00:02:18,910
Jonathon: So I'm going to do it, and
you can fix whatever I get wrong.

43
00:02:19,336 --> 00:02:19,950
Wes Hart: Go ahead.

44
00:02:21,179 --> 00:02:25,370
Jonathon: Okay, so. This is Wes
Hart. He's fifty-two years old.

45
00:02:25,716 --> 00:02:30,720
Jonathon: Wes lives out in the Hill Country, west
of San Antonio, on a few acres, in a town he

46
00:02:30,730 --> 00:02:32,186
won't let me say on the air.

47
00:02:32,543 --> 00:02:33,043
Wes Hart: Correct.

48
00:02:33,330 --> 00:02:36,630
Jonathon: He's been married twenty-seven
years. He's got three kids.

49
00:02:36,790 --> 00:02:40,200
A daughter who's twenty-five,
married, teaches second grade.

50
00:02:40,210 --> 00:02:44,280
A son who's twenty-one, finishing
up an electrical apprenticeship.

51
00:02:44,290 --> 00:02:49,035
And a sixteen-year-old who just got his
license, which is why Wes looks like that.

52
00:02:49,290 --> 00:02:53,310
Wes Hart: He drives like he's late for
something. He's never late for anything.

53
00:02:53,610 --> 00:02:56,850
He's got nowhere to be.
He just drives like it.

54
00:02:57,127 --> 00:02:58,097
Jonathon: You rode with him?

55
00:02:58,410 --> 00:02:59,790
Wes Hart: I rode with him Saturday.

56
00:03:00,150 --> 00:03:03,550
I held the handle above the door
the whole way to the feed store.

57
00:03:03,790 --> 00:03:07,162
The handle's there for a
reason. I found the reason.

58
00:03:07,670 --> 00:03:11,502
Jonathon: And what does Wes do for a
living? I've known this man seven years.

59
00:03:11,622 --> 00:03:12,548
Wes Hart: I work.

60
00:03:12,805 --> 00:03:13,770
Jonathon: He works.

61
00:03:13,904 --> 00:03:14,800
Wes Hart: Same as you.

62
00:03:14,810 --> 00:03:19,980
I get up early, I come home tired,
my truck has too many miles on it.

63
00:03:19,990 --> 00:03:21,883
That's all anybody needs.

64
00:03:22,117 --> 00:03:23,540
Jonathon: That's going to be a running thing.

65
00:03:23,550 --> 00:03:25,508
People are going to ask in the comments.

66
00:03:25,813 --> 00:03:27,070
Wes Hart: Let them ask.

67
00:03:27,238 --> 00:03:30,060
Jonathon: Okay. Here's the
part that matters, though.

68
00:03:30,070 --> 00:03:34,320
For twenty-something years, Wes has
been sitting across tables from men.

69
00:03:34,330 --> 00:03:38,270
Kitchen tables, church
fellowship halls, deer camp.

70
00:03:38,389 --> 00:03:41,670
Wes Hart: Deer camp's the
best one. Nobody can leave.

71
00:03:41,792 --> 00:03:45,820
Jonathon: And a couple years ago he wrote down
what he'd learned at all those tables, and

72
00:03:45,830 --> 00:03:50,510
that's the book. We'll get to the
book. That's my version. Now fix it.

73
00:03:50,544 --> 00:03:51,700
Wes Hart: That's mostly right.

74
00:03:51,710 --> 00:03:55,570
You left out the part where I wasn't
any good at this for a long time.

75
00:03:55,713 --> 00:03:56,970
Jonathon: I was getting there.

76
00:03:57,077 --> 00:04:02,420
Wes Hart: You were being nice. I grew up on
my granddad's place. My dad was a steady man.

77
00:04:02,430 --> 00:04:03,080
Quiet.

78
00:04:03,090 --> 00:04:07,030
He was at every game I ever played,
and he almost never said what he felt.

79
00:04:07,450 --> 00:04:10,410
I loved him. He loved me. I knew that.

80
00:04:10,430 --> 00:04:14,800
But the first time I heard him say "I'm
proud of you," he was in a hospital bed, and

81
00:04:14,810 --> 00:04:15,970
I was forty-three.

82
00:04:17,050 --> 00:04:19,080
Wes Hart: He died nine years ago.

83
00:04:19,090 --> 00:04:23,190
And I want to be careful here, because
that's not a sad story about a bad dad.

84
00:04:23,210 --> 00:04:26,360
He was a good dad. It's
a different kind of ache.

85
00:04:26,370 --> 00:04:30,410
A good man who never said it out loud.
I've met a lot of men with that one.

86
00:04:30,570 --> 00:04:31,210
Jonathon: Yeah.

87
00:04:31,479 --> 00:04:32,730
Wes Hart: And then my thirties.

88
00:04:32,830 --> 00:04:37,380
I had a decent job, I coached Little League,
I sat in the third row at church every

89
00:04:37,390 --> 00:04:42,470
Sunday. And I was gone. In my own living
room. My wife has a name for those years.

90
00:04:42,792 --> 00:04:44,150
Jonathon: The fog years.

91
00:04:44,584 --> 00:04:49,150
Wes Hart: The fog years. She saw it before
I did. I want that on the record early.

92
00:04:49,230 --> 00:04:52,890
She saw it first, and she was
patient a lot longer than I deserved.

93
00:04:53,396 --> 00:04:55,950
Wes Hart: And I know that's
not how it goes for every man.

94
00:04:55,990 --> 00:04:58,730
Some of you, she stopped
asking, and then she left.

95
00:04:59,030 --> 00:05:03,390
I'm not going to pretend my ending is the
only one. You've got a chair here too.

96
00:05:03,635 --> 00:05:05,570
Jonathon: And then the man with the mug.

97
00:05:06,635 --> 00:05:08,320
Wes Hart: And then the man with the mug.

98
00:05:08,330 --> 00:05:13,720
When I was thirty-six, I went to a men's
breakfast I didn't want to go to, and there

99
00:05:13,730 --> 00:05:18,640
was an older man there. I call
him Mr. Abernathy. Everybody did.

100
00:05:18,650 --> 00:05:22,120
He'd been a lineman for the
power company forty years.

101
00:05:22,130 --> 00:05:26,860
And he looked across that table at me, didn't
know me from anybody, and asked me one

102
00:05:26,870 --> 00:05:27,785
question.

103
00:05:28,048 --> 00:05:29,630
Wes Hart: "What are you waiting on?"

104
00:05:30,594 --> 00:05:35,302
Wes Hart: I didn't have an answer. I had
a lot of words. I didn't have an answer.

105
00:05:35,513 --> 00:05:37,230
Jonathon: And he didn't let it go.

106
00:05:37,445 --> 00:05:41,080
Wes Hart: He met me every other
Thursday for six years. Same diner.

107
00:05:41,090 --> 00:05:45,880
He drank his coffee out of this exact mug,
and when he died at eighty-eight, the

108
00:05:45,890 --> 00:05:49,820
woman who ran the place wrapped it in a
paper towel and handed it to me at the

109
00:05:49,830 --> 00:05:50,564
funeral.

110
00:05:50,792 --> 00:05:52,170
Jonathon: I didn't know that part.

111
00:05:52,370 --> 00:05:55,750
Wes Hart: I don't tell it
much. So that's who I am.

112
00:05:55,890 --> 00:06:00,140
I'm a man who wasted some years and got
pulled out of it by an old lineman who kept

113
00:06:00,150 --> 00:06:01,450
asking a question.

114
00:06:01,747 --> 00:06:05,803
Jonathon: So why are you doing this? Why a
show? You could just keep doing the tables.

115
00:06:06,202 --> 00:06:08,810
Wes Hart: Because most
men never get to a table.

116
00:06:08,950 --> 00:06:13,460
I've had the same conversation with hundreds
of men, and for every one who sat down,

117
00:06:13,470 --> 00:06:16,310
there's ten who would have,
if they'd known it existed.

118
00:06:16,510 --> 00:06:20,310
And because I'm fifty-two now, and
the man who sat with me is gone.

119
00:06:20,410 --> 00:06:22,349
Somebody has to pull up the chair.

120
00:06:22,763 --> 00:06:24,178
Jonathon: And what do you bring?

121
00:06:24,570 --> 00:06:26,390
Wes Hart: The long view, I guess.

122
00:06:26,750 --> 00:06:30,740
I've been married twenty-seven years, so
I've been wrong in most of the ways you can

123
00:06:30,750 --> 00:06:32,110
be wrong in a marriage.

124
00:06:32,410 --> 00:06:36,310
I've raised three kids, and I've
been wrong in all of those ways too.

125
00:06:36,630 --> 00:06:41,230
If you're in a season, odds are I've been
through it or sat with a man who was.

126
00:06:41,450 --> 00:06:44,696
And I'll tell you when I got
it wrong. That's the deal.

127
00:06:45,110 --> 00:06:47,838
Jonathon: He will. He tells
on himself constantly.

128
00:06:48,012 --> 00:06:50,350
Wes Hart: It's the only
thing I'm consistent at.

129
00:06:51,555 --> 00:06:53,113
Wes Hart: Now. My turn.

130
00:06:53,470 --> 00:06:54,210
Jonathon: Be nice.

131
00:06:54,490 --> 00:06:55,735
Wes Hart: I'm always nice.

132
00:06:56,124 --> 00:06:58,054
Jonathon: You are not always nice.

133
00:06:58,547 --> 00:06:59,650
Wes Hart: This is Jonathon.

134
00:07:00,350 --> 00:07:06,059
He's thirty-six, thirty-seven in
February. He grew up in Pasadena, Texas.

135
00:07:06,490 --> 00:07:07,310
Jonathon: Here we go.

136
00:07:07,658 --> 00:07:11,870
Wes Hart: Pasadena, Texas, for those of
you not from here, is east of Houston.

137
00:07:11,970 --> 00:07:15,717
Refinery country. Flat. It smells like work.

138
00:07:16,150 --> 00:07:20,910
Jonathon: It's a great place. Nothing's the
Hill Country, I know. Everyone's been told.

139
00:07:21,299 --> 00:07:25,321
Wes Hart: He lives on the north side
of San Antonio now, in a subdivision.

140
00:07:25,773 --> 00:07:29,880
Wes Hart: Jonathon is a regional sales manager
for a company that sells commercial air

141
00:07:29,890 --> 00:07:31,350
conditioning equipment.

142
00:07:31,708 --> 00:07:34,170
Jonathon: Which in Texas is
the easiest pitch in the world.

143
00:07:34,370 --> 00:07:36,347
"Would you like to not die in August?"

144
00:07:36,770 --> 00:07:38,850
Wes Hart: He's been married ten years to Dani.

145
00:07:39,190 --> 00:07:43,360
She's a labor and delivery nurse,
and she works nights, three a week.

146
00:07:43,630 --> 00:07:46,930
Jonathon: She's the best person I
know. You can put that on a card.

147
00:07:47,247 --> 00:07:48,850
Wes Hart: And they've got two kids.

148
00:07:49,150 --> 00:07:54,310
Ruby, who's seven and runs the house, and
Theo, who's four and runs into things.

149
00:07:54,776 --> 00:07:56,821
Jonathon: That's accurate. That is accurate.

150
00:07:57,273 --> 00:08:00,850
Wes Hart: He pitched in college,
small school, until his shoulder went.

151
00:08:01,130 --> 00:08:03,630
Now he coaches Ruby's coach-pitch team.

152
00:08:03,872 --> 00:08:05,470
Jonathon: Badly. Say it.

153
00:08:05,630 --> 00:08:06,953
Wes Hart: With a lot of energy.

154
00:08:07,322 --> 00:08:10,850
Jonathon: Last fall we went
oh-and-six. We've never won a game.

155
00:08:11,070 --> 00:08:14,720
Ruby picked this watch for me at the end
of the season, and it does not tell time

156
00:08:14,730 --> 00:08:15,442
correctly.

157
00:08:15,680 --> 00:08:16,995
Wes Hart: Does it beep?

158
00:08:17,019 --> 00:08:20,599
Jonathon: Every hour. On the
hour. It's beeped in church.

159
00:08:20,999 --> 00:08:23,370
Wes Hart: That's my version. Fix it.

160
00:08:23,390 --> 00:08:28,300
Jonathon: Okay. That's the fun part. Here's
the rest of it. My dad left when I was nine.

161
00:08:28,310 --> 00:08:30,360
It was a Saturday, in October.

162
00:08:30,370 --> 00:08:34,900
I remember his truck had a dent in the
tailgate, and I remember watching the dent go

163
00:08:34,910 --> 00:08:39,330
down the street. That's the thing
I remember. Not his face. The dent.

164
00:08:40,474 --> 00:08:44,540
Jonathon: He's alive. He lives in Lake
Charles. He calls about twice a year.

165
00:08:44,550 --> 00:08:48,330
And I'm going to tell you right up
front, I have not figured that out.

166
00:08:48,510 --> 00:08:51,960
We're going to talk about dads on this
show, and I'm going to be the guy who hasn't

167
00:08:51,970 --> 00:08:52,950
healed on schedule.

168
00:08:53,271 --> 00:08:54,549
Wes Hart: That's allowed here.

169
00:08:54,742 --> 00:08:59,700
Jonathon: My mom worked two jobs, and by eleven
she was calling me the man of the house.

170
00:08:59,710 --> 00:09:01,410
She meant it as a compliment.

171
00:09:01,490 --> 00:09:04,980
She's a great woman, she did
everything right that she could do.

172
00:09:04,990 --> 00:09:07,752
But eleven's young to be the man of anything.

173
00:09:08,085 --> 00:09:08,826
Wes Hart: It is.

174
00:09:09,186 --> 00:09:13,330
Jonathon: And then my twenties. I
was good at sales. Like, really good.

175
00:09:13,610 --> 00:09:16,360
I was good at the room. I
lived for the leaderboard.

176
00:09:16,370 --> 00:09:21,270
And I drank too much on weeknights to
come down from the day. Nothing dramatic.

177
00:09:21,290 --> 00:09:23,000
No arrests, no wrecks.

178
00:09:23,010 --> 00:09:27,346
Just every night, a little too
much, and I called it unwinding.

179
00:09:27,606 --> 00:09:29,000
Wes Hart: When did that turn?

180
00:09:29,330 --> 00:09:31,540
Jonathon: At twenty-nine.
Dani was pregnant with Ruby.

181
00:09:31,550 --> 00:09:35,520
And she sat down across from me at our
kitchen table one night, and she was so calm

182
00:09:35,530 --> 00:09:39,650
about it, which was the scary part.
She said, "I'm not raising two kids."

183
00:09:39,790 --> 00:09:40,495
Wes Hart: Yeah.

184
00:09:40,826 --> 00:09:41,960
Jonathon: And she wasn't wrong.

185
00:09:41,970 --> 00:09:47,120
About a month later, a customer of mine,
a mechanical contractor, invited me to a

186
00:09:47,130 --> 00:09:48,380
men's breakfast at his church.

187
00:09:48,390 --> 00:09:51,930
And I went because I couldn't come
up with an excuse fast enough.

188
00:09:52,191 --> 00:09:54,938
Wes Hart: That's how most men
end up at a men's breakfast.

189
00:09:55,287 --> 00:09:57,440
Jonathon: I sat in the parking
lot for eleven minutes.

190
00:09:57,450 --> 00:10:01,360
I know because I watched the clock
on the dash. And I almost left.

191
00:10:01,370 --> 00:10:05,380
And when I finally went in, there was one
open seat, and it was across from this

192
00:10:05,390 --> 00:10:05,910
guy.

193
00:10:06,068 --> 00:10:09,470
Wes Hart: I remember you. You
had a company quarter-zip on.

194
00:10:09,797 --> 00:10:11,070
Jonathon: I still have it.

195
00:10:11,250 --> 00:10:13,777
Wes Hart: I know. You wear it on this show.

196
00:10:14,063 --> 00:10:15,400
Jonathon: I'm going to wear it on this show.

197
00:10:15,410 --> 00:10:17,790
And I didn't say anything to him that morning.

198
00:10:18,110 --> 00:10:21,380
Three months later I sent him a text
asking if he'd get breakfast with me.

199
00:10:21,390 --> 00:10:24,243
That is the scariest text
I've ever sent a man.

200
00:10:24,565 --> 00:10:29,270
Wes Hart: It said, "Hey, this is Jonathon from
the breakfast, the HVAC guy, no pressure."

201
00:10:29,690 --> 00:10:31,570
Jonathon: "No pressure"
means a lot of pressure.

202
00:10:32,007 --> 00:10:33,290
Wes Hart: It means a lot of pressure.

203
00:10:33,614 --> 00:10:37,350
Jonathon: And that was seven years
ago. We've met most Thursdays since.

204
00:10:37,550 --> 00:10:38,780
I came to faith that year.

205
00:10:38,790 --> 00:10:41,550
And I haven't had a
weeknight drink in six years.

206
00:10:41,870 --> 00:10:45,930
Nothing dramatic about that either.
I just quit, and I had a guy to tell.

207
00:10:46,270 --> 00:10:48,070
Wes Hart: So why are you doing this?

208
00:10:48,430 --> 00:10:50,880
Jonathon: Because this show isn't
for men who have it together.

209
00:10:50,890 --> 00:10:55,180
It's for the man I was at twenty-nine,
sitting in that parking lot, watching the

210
00:10:55,190 --> 00:10:57,350
clock, deciding whether to go in.

211
00:10:57,370 --> 00:11:00,710
If that's you, I want you to
know somebody's at the table.

212
00:11:01,070 --> 00:11:03,850
And because I'm still
learning. I'm seven years in.

213
00:11:03,890 --> 00:11:06,490
I'm proof this works for
a guy who started late.

214
00:11:06,841 --> 00:11:07,918
Wes Hart: And what do you bring?

215
00:11:08,345 --> 00:11:10,690
Jonathon: Questions. I'm
going to push back on him.

216
00:11:10,770 --> 00:11:14,400
If he says something that sounds great at
a breakfast and wouldn't survive my house

217
00:11:14,410 --> 00:11:16,990
at six-fifteen, I'm going to say so.

218
00:11:17,350 --> 00:11:18,154
Wes Hart: He will.

219
00:11:18,450 --> 00:11:21,110
Jonathon: I'm the guy in the
back. That's my job here.

220
00:11:21,237 --> 00:11:22,413
Wes Hart: That's a good job.

221
00:11:23,811 --> 00:11:27,520
Wes Hart: So the first chapter of the book I
wrote, and we'll tell you about the book later,

222
00:11:27,530 --> 00:11:31,770
is called "A Quiet Tuesday."
And the idea is simple.

223
00:11:32,330 --> 00:11:38,510
Most men are measured by their highlight
days. The wedding. The promotion. The trip.

224
00:11:38,510 --> 00:11:42,670
The game where you were the
guy. And those days matter.

225
00:11:42,950 --> 00:11:47,330
But that's not where a man gets
made. A man gets made on Tuesday.

226
00:11:47,750 --> 00:11:49,748
The day nobody posts.

227
00:11:50,103 --> 00:11:52,087
Jonathon: Which is why we
launched on a Tuesday.

228
00:11:52,570 --> 00:11:56,190
Wes Hart: Which is why we launched on
a Tuesday. That wasn't an accident.

229
00:11:56,598 --> 00:11:58,008
Jonathon: I thought that was an accident.

230
00:11:58,470 --> 00:11:59,910
Wes Hart: It was a happy accident.

231
00:12:00,399 --> 00:12:06,090
Wes Hart: And if this episode has one
plain point, it's this. Stop waiting.

232
00:12:06,650 --> 00:12:08,590
That's the mark we're on today.

233
00:12:08,910 --> 00:12:14,480
A lot of us are waiting to become a man we
keep describing. "When things slow down."

234
00:12:14,490 --> 00:12:19,948
"When the kids are older." "When work
settles." And the waiting is the problem.

235
00:12:20,350 --> 00:12:21,000
Jonathon: Okay, so.

236
00:12:21,010 --> 00:12:25,880
Can I just tell the 1604 thing properly?
Because I gave the trailer version.

237
00:12:26,350 --> 00:12:27,070
Wes Hart: Tell it.

238
00:12:27,446 --> 00:12:31,670
Jonathon: Ruby was four, Theo was one,
and Dani had just gone back to nights.

239
00:12:31,930 --> 00:12:36,050
So three nights a week, I've got both of
them by myself after the sitter leaves.

240
00:12:36,390 --> 00:12:38,590
And six o'clock at our house is chaos.

241
00:12:39,210 --> 00:12:43,470
Dinner, two baths, one of them screaming,
the whole fight about pajamas.

242
00:12:43,590 --> 00:12:45,980
And I'm coming off a day
of being good at the room.

243
00:12:45,990 --> 00:12:49,620
I'm on the phone with customers,
I'm closing, everybody likes me.

244
00:12:49,630 --> 00:12:54,031
And I'd drive home and I'd feel it in
my chest around the exit. The chaos.

245
00:12:54,575 --> 00:12:58,150
Jonathon: So one night I just
missed my exit. On purpose.

246
00:12:58,330 --> 00:13:02,500
Got on 1604 at six-fifteen,
which nobody does on purpose.

247
00:13:02,510 --> 00:13:04,540
Added twenty minutes of brake lights.

248
00:13:04,550 --> 00:13:08,620
And when I walked in, the sitter had
already done baths, and both kids were in

249
00:13:08,630 --> 00:13:13,170
pajamas, and I got the good part. The
story, the hug, the little prayer.

250
00:13:13,250 --> 00:13:14,750
I got the highlight.

251
00:13:15,014 --> 00:13:16,495
Wes Hart: And skipped the Tuesday.

252
00:13:16,858 --> 00:13:19,310
Jonathon: I skipped the
Tuesday. For two years.

253
00:13:19,730 --> 00:13:22,390
And here's the part I didn't
do the math on until later.

254
00:13:22,650 --> 00:13:24,490
We paid that sitter by the hour.

255
00:13:24,650 --> 00:13:28,890
So I was paying somebody by the hour
so I could sit in traffic on purpose.

256
00:13:29,250 --> 00:13:34,470
I told myself I needed the decompression.
And Dani's a nurse. She deals in facts.

257
00:13:34,730 --> 00:13:37,670
She just asked me since when I liked 1604.

258
00:13:37,850 --> 00:13:39,016
Wes Hart: What did you say?

259
00:13:39,499 --> 00:13:45,420
Jonathon: I said, "It's not that bad." And she
said, "Okay." And she didn't say anything else.

260
00:13:45,430 --> 00:13:48,624
And that "okay" followed me
around for about a week.

261
00:13:49,390 --> 00:13:51,350
Wes Hart: I got an okay like that once.

262
00:13:51,695 --> 00:13:52,935
Jonathon: You got the okay?

263
00:13:53,190 --> 00:13:55,250
Wes Hart: Here's where I
have to tell on myself.

264
00:13:55,730 --> 00:14:01,650
In the fog years, I did all the big
things. I built a deck. I coached.

265
00:14:02,030 --> 00:14:06,210
I took the family to the coast every
summer, and I'd plan it for months.

266
00:14:06,690 --> 00:14:09,710
The pictures from those
trips are great pictures.

267
00:14:10,110 --> 00:14:14,940
And then on a normal Tuesday, I'd come home
and eat supper in the recliner with the

268
00:14:14,950 --> 00:14:15,810
news on.

269
00:14:16,210 --> 00:14:17,295
Jonathon: In the recliner.

270
00:14:17,431 --> 00:14:18,500
Wes Hart: In the recliner.

271
00:14:18,510 --> 00:14:21,500
And the family's at the
table, fifteen feet away.

272
00:14:21,510 --> 00:14:25,000
And for a while my wife would
call me over. "Come sit with us."

273
00:14:25,010 --> 00:14:26,620
And I'd say, "In a minute."

274
00:14:26,630 --> 00:14:29,513
And then one night, I
noticed she'd stopped asking.

275
00:14:30,756 --> 00:14:33,600
Wes Hart: She'd stopped asking. That's
when I should have been scared.

276
00:14:33,610 --> 00:14:36,450
Not when she was asking. When she stopped.

277
00:14:38,028 --> 00:14:39,598
Jonathon: What made you go to the table?

278
00:14:39,801 --> 00:14:43,440
Wes Hart: Honestly? Nothing
heroic. My daughter was about nine.

279
00:14:43,450 --> 00:14:47,140
And she brought her plate over to the
recliner and sat on the floor next to it.

280
00:14:47,150 --> 00:14:49,170
Because that's where her dad was.

281
00:14:49,551 --> 00:14:50,411
Jonathon: Oh, man.

282
00:14:50,898 --> 00:14:54,280
Wes Hart: Yeah. She wasn't making a point.
She just wanted to eat with her dad.

283
00:14:54,290 --> 00:14:58,320
And I looked down at her on the floor,
eating off her lap, and I thought, what am I

284
00:14:58,330 --> 00:15:00,630
doing? What am I waiting on?

285
00:15:00,730 --> 00:15:03,070
Which is funny, because
that's the question Mr.

286
00:15:03,250 --> 00:15:06,520
Abernathy asked me about a year
later. I'd already heard it.

287
00:15:06,530 --> 00:15:08,254
I just hadn't answered it.

288
00:15:09,331 --> 00:15:12,790
Wes Hart: There's a line in First
Corinthians that I can't get away from.

289
00:15:13,010 --> 00:15:17,340
It's in chapter thirteen, which most people
know as the love chapter, because it

290
00:15:17,350 --> 00:15:19,141
gets read at every wedding.

291
00:15:19,509 --> 00:15:20,190
Jonathon: Ours.

292
00:15:20,518 --> 00:15:21,650
Wes Hart: Everybody's.

293
00:15:21,770 --> 00:15:23,975
And in the middle of it, Paul says this.

294
00:15:24,435 --> 00:15:29,480
Wes Hart: "When I was a child, I talked like a
child, I thought like a child, I reasoned like

295
00:15:29,490 --> 00:15:30,290
a child.

296
00:15:30,490 --> 00:15:34,590
When I became a man, I put the
ways of childhood behind me."

297
00:15:34,890 --> 00:15:37,190
Wes Hart: And here's the
context in two sentences.

298
00:15:37,650 --> 00:15:40,830
Paul's writing to a church in
Corinth that had everything.

299
00:15:41,430 --> 00:15:43,300
Gifts, talent, big moments.

300
00:15:43,310 --> 00:15:48,700
And they were acting like kids with it,
everybody chasing the impressive stuff and

301
00:15:48,710 --> 00:15:51,810
nobody loving anybody on a regular day.

302
00:15:52,058 --> 00:15:54,390
Jonathon: So, a church full of guys like me.

303
00:15:54,712 --> 00:15:58,430
Wes Hart: A church full of highlight
reels. And look at the verb.

304
00:15:58,810 --> 00:16:04,190
"I put the ways of childhood behind
me." He did it. It's not a birthday.

305
00:16:04,610 --> 00:16:08,904
You don't turn thirty and wake up
a man. You put something down.

306
00:16:09,132 --> 00:16:10,312
Jonathon: What's the something?

307
00:16:10,630 --> 00:16:13,850
Wes Hart: For me, it was the
recliner. That's not a joke.

308
00:16:14,390 --> 00:16:17,810
The recliner was where I went
to be a kid on a Tuesday.

309
00:16:18,310 --> 00:16:23,470
Nobody needs anything from me, the TV's
talking, I don't have to be present.

310
00:16:23,870 --> 00:16:28,270
That was childhood. That was
me reasoning like a child.

311
00:16:28,612 --> 00:16:30,930
Jonathon: And for me it was 1604.

312
00:16:31,190 --> 00:16:33,482
Wes Hart: For you it was 1604.

313
00:16:33,890 --> 00:16:39,240
Wes Hart: And then Jesus says something in Luke
sixteen that I think is the whole Tuesday idea

314
00:16:39,250 --> 00:16:40,420
in one sentence.

315
00:16:40,430 --> 00:16:45,690
He's telling a story about a manager
and money, and he stops and says this.

316
00:16:46,100 --> 00:16:51,680
Wes Hart: "Whoever can be trusted with very
little can also be trusted with much, and whoever

317
00:16:51,690 --> 00:16:56,468
is dishonest with very little will
also be dishonest with much."

318
00:16:56,928 --> 00:17:03,550
Wes Hart: Tuesday is the very little. It's
six-fifteen. It's supper. It's the bath fight.

319
00:17:03,950 --> 00:17:08,040
And the way you handle the very little
is the way you handle the much.

320
00:17:08,050 --> 00:17:13,060
You don't get to be one man on the trip
to the coast and a different man in the

321
00:17:13,070 --> 00:17:18,449
recliner. It's the same man. The
trip just has better lighting.

322
00:17:19,437 --> 00:17:22,687
Jonathon: That's annoying. I
have a lot of trip pictures.

323
00:17:23,237 --> 00:17:27,290
Jonathon: Quick thing. If this is helping,
hit subscribe. We do this twice a week.

324
00:17:27,710 --> 00:17:29,914
Wes Hart: He means it. He checks.

325
00:17:30,289 --> 00:17:32,810
Jonathon: I check every
hour. When the watch beeps.

326
00:17:34,284 --> 00:17:36,530
Jonathon: Okay, so here's my
problem with all of this.

327
00:17:36,766 --> 00:17:37,933
Wes Hart: Here it comes.

328
00:17:38,380 --> 00:17:40,470
Jonathon: Some Tuesdays I'm just tired.

329
00:17:40,930 --> 00:17:44,820
Dani's on nights, I've had the kids
since five, Theo's been sticky since

330
00:17:44,830 --> 00:17:45,690
five-oh-two.

331
00:17:46,170 --> 00:17:49,330
Is it a sin to sit in the car
for ten minutes before I go in?

332
00:17:49,570 --> 00:17:53,580
Because I'm going to be honest, some
days the car is the only quiet I get.

333
00:17:53,864 --> 00:17:58,346
Wes Hart: No. Ten minutes in the
car isn't a sin. Rest isn't hiding.

334
00:17:58,817 --> 00:18:03,077
Jonathon: Okay, then what's the
difference? Because 1604 was me resting.

335
00:18:03,510 --> 00:18:04,190
Wes Hart: Was it?

336
00:18:05,043 --> 00:18:05,773
Jonathon: No.

337
00:18:05,908 --> 00:18:09,230
Wes Hart: Here's the test I use.
When you rest, you come back.

338
00:18:09,590 --> 00:18:12,150
You come back to the people,
and you come back better.

339
00:18:12,470 --> 00:18:15,960
When you hide, you're trying to make sure
you don't come back until the hard part's

340
00:18:15,970 --> 00:18:16,550
over.

341
00:18:16,854 --> 00:18:20,810
Jonathon: So ten minutes in the car out front
with the engine off, breathing, praying,

342
00:18:20,910 --> 00:18:23,259
whatever, and then I go in and do bath.

343
00:18:23,650 --> 00:18:24,787
Wes Hart: That's rest.

344
00:18:25,270 --> 00:18:29,130
Jonathon: And fifteen minutes on 1604
so that somebody else does bath.

345
00:18:29,450 --> 00:18:30,371
Wes Hart: That's hiding.

346
00:18:30,833 --> 00:18:32,410
Jonathon: Okay. I buy that.

347
00:18:32,570 --> 00:18:35,970
But I've got a second problem, and I
don't think you're going to like this one.

348
00:18:36,160 --> 00:18:37,664
Wes Hart: I haven't liked the first one.

349
00:18:37,956 --> 00:18:39,970
Jonathon: I think you undersell the big days.

350
00:18:40,270 --> 00:18:44,310
You keep saying the trip has better
lighting, like the trip doesn't count.

351
00:18:44,710 --> 00:18:49,550
Ruby still talks about the fishing trip we
took to Port Aransas. We caught one fish.

352
00:18:49,730 --> 00:18:54,570
A hardhead catfish. It stung me.
She talks about it every week.

353
00:18:54,950 --> 00:18:55,912
Wes Hart: She should.

354
00:18:56,190 --> 00:18:59,696
Jonathon: So the big days matter.
Kids remember the big days.

355
00:18:59,950 --> 00:19:04,250
Wes Hart: They do. I'll give you that.
I'm not saying throw out the big days.

356
00:19:04,650 --> 00:19:07,730
My kids remember the
coast. But here's my push.

357
00:19:08,090 --> 00:19:11,420
Your daughter remembers the fishing trip
because you were there for her on a hundred

358
00:19:11,430 --> 00:19:12,770
Tuesdays around it.

359
00:19:12,950 --> 00:19:16,740
If you'd been gone on all the Tuesdays,
the fishing trip would be a nice picture of

360
00:19:16,750 --> 00:19:18,630
a guy she doesn't know very well.

361
00:19:19,440 --> 00:19:20,544
Jonathon: That's not fair.

362
00:19:20,912 --> 00:19:21,649
Wes Hart: Why not?

363
00:19:21,890 --> 00:19:24,030
Jonathon: Because it's true
and I can't argue with it.

364
00:19:24,290 --> 00:19:28,210
Jonathon: But I still think you undersell
Saturdays. I'm putting that on the record.

365
00:19:28,350 --> 00:19:31,660
Episode one. I think the
big days are part of it.

366
00:19:31,710 --> 00:19:34,474
Wes Hart: They're part of
it. They're built on Tuesday.

367
00:19:34,756 --> 00:19:36,050
Jonathon: We'll come back to this.

368
00:19:36,228 --> 00:19:37,661
Wes Hart: We'll come back to it.

369
00:19:37,943 --> 00:19:39,570
Jonathon: Okay. Third thing.

370
00:19:39,630 --> 00:19:44,040
And this is for the guy who's listening
and going, I don't have a 1604, because

371
00:19:44,050 --> 00:19:45,550
nobody's at my house.

372
00:19:45,853 --> 00:19:46,505
Wes Hart: Good.

373
00:19:46,970 --> 00:19:51,991
Jonathon: The twenty-two-year-old in an
apartment. No wife, no kids. What's his Tuesday?

374
00:19:52,470 --> 00:19:56,260
Wes Hart: His Tuesday is his whole life
right now, not practice for some other one.

375
00:19:56,270 --> 00:20:00,870
Is he the guy who shows up when a buddy's
moving, or the one who's suddenly busy?

376
00:20:01,250 --> 00:20:04,130
Does he do his job like it
matters when the boss is gone?

377
00:20:04,510 --> 00:20:06,350
Does his money go anywhere on purpose?

378
00:20:06,710 --> 00:20:09,910
Is there one man who knows what's
actually going on with him?

379
00:20:10,210 --> 00:20:13,510
Does he open the Book on a
Tuesday, or does Sunday cover it?

380
00:20:13,790 --> 00:20:16,090
Jonathon: That's a lot of questions
for a twenty-two-year-old.

381
00:20:16,510 --> 00:20:17,710
Wes Hart: He can handle them.

382
00:20:18,070 --> 00:20:20,970
Twenty-two-year-olds run
crews and fly airplanes.

383
00:20:21,430 --> 00:20:26,230
Whether there's ever a wedding or not,
the man he is on Tuesday is the man he is.

384
00:20:26,438 --> 00:20:30,028
Jonathon: And the guy on the other
end? Sixty-two, kids are grown.

385
00:20:30,511 --> 00:20:33,830
Wes Hart: I'm not going to tell a
sixty-two-year-old what his season is.

386
00:20:34,230 --> 00:20:38,780
I'm fifty-two, and the men I know who are
sixty-two are starting businesses, going

387
00:20:38,790 --> 00:20:43,470
back to school, still coaching, still in
a fight with a brother they need to end.

388
00:20:43,950 --> 00:20:47,690
His question's the same as
mine. What's he waiting on?

389
00:20:47,990 --> 00:20:51,980
And with twenty good years in front of him,
he's got more room to answer it than I

390
00:20:51,990 --> 00:20:52,962
did at thirty.

391
00:20:53,270 --> 00:20:57,510
Jonathon: Okay, last one, and this one's
me. What about the guy who's great at work?

392
00:20:57,670 --> 00:21:02,350
Because my problem was never that I was
lazy. At work, every day is a Saturday.

393
00:21:02,570 --> 00:21:03,430
I'm the guy.

394
00:21:03,670 --> 00:21:07,800
I hit the number, they put my name on the
board, my phone lights up because people

395
00:21:07,810 --> 00:21:08,650
need me.

396
00:21:08,984 --> 00:21:10,450
Wes Hart: And then you drive home.

397
00:21:10,710 --> 00:21:14,450
Jonathon: And then I drive home, and
nobody at my house cares about the board.

398
00:21:14,810 --> 00:21:19,152
Ruby doesn't know what a quarterly number
is. Theo wants me to be a dinosaur.

399
00:21:19,630 --> 00:21:21,122
Wes Hart: What kind of dinosaur?

400
00:21:21,333 --> 00:21:24,333
Jonathon: It changes. It's
usually the one that gets eaten.

401
00:21:24,733 --> 00:21:29,240
Wes Hart: That man is the hardest one to reach,
and I say that with love, because I've sat

402
00:21:29,250 --> 00:21:33,210
with a lot of him. He's not
checked out. He's spent.

403
00:21:33,750 --> 00:21:37,800
He gave the good version of himself to people
who sign his check, and the people who

404
00:21:37,810 --> 00:21:42,030
share his last name get what's
left. And he doesn't feel passive.

405
00:21:42,430 --> 00:21:43,750
He feels exhausted.

406
00:21:44,170 --> 00:21:46,826
He'll tell you he's the
hardest worker he knows.

407
00:21:47,017 --> 00:21:48,390
Jonathon: I would have told you that.

408
00:21:48,670 --> 00:21:53,160
Wes Hart: And it'd be true. And it's
still the recliner. It's a nicer recliner.

409
00:21:53,170 --> 00:21:55,657
It's got a company logo on it.

410
00:21:55,667 --> 00:21:58,876
Jonathon: Yeah. That one's
going to bother me tonight.

411
00:21:58,886 --> 00:22:00,761
Wes Hart: Good. Let it.

412
00:22:01,826 --> 00:22:05,640
Jonathon: Okay. So what did you
actually do? Not then. This week.

413
00:22:05,650 --> 00:22:09,510
Because we recorded this on a Thursday
and you've known the topic for a month.

414
00:22:09,541 --> 00:22:10,220
Wes Hart: This week.

415
00:22:10,230 --> 00:22:15,020
I put my phone in the console of my truck
before supper. Three nights out of five.

416
00:22:15,030 --> 00:22:20,260
And the reason it was three and not five
is that on Tuesday, of all days, my

417
00:22:20,270 --> 00:22:24,160
youngest had a question about his car
insurance, and I took the phone to the table

418
00:22:24,170 --> 00:22:27,661
to look it up, and then I was looking
at something else for twenty minutes.

419
00:22:28,105 --> 00:22:29,370
Jonathon: What were you looking at?

420
00:22:30,310 --> 00:22:32,879
Wes Hart: A video of a
man restoring a tractor.

421
00:22:33,250 --> 00:22:34,461
Jonathon: For twenty minutes.

422
00:22:34,770 --> 00:22:36,117
Wes Hart: It was a good tractor.

423
00:22:36,523 --> 00:22:38,830
Jonathon: So you were the
recliner. At the table.

424
00:22:39,290 --> 00:22:43,530
Wes Hart: I brought the recliner to the
table. Yes. That's what the phone is.

425
00:22:43,630 --> 00:22:45,699
It's a recliner you can carry.

426
00:22:46,065 --> 00:22:50,130
Wes Hart: I'll say that again because I think
it's the most useful thing I'll say today.

427
00:22:50,450 --> 00:22:55,174
The phone is a recliner you can carry.
You can be at the table and gone.

428
00:22:55,644 --> 00:22:56,590
Wes Hart: What about you?

429
00:22:57,080 --> 00:23:01,260
Jonathon: I drove the direct route home.
Every night Dani worked. Three nights.

430
00:23:01,648 --> 00:23:02,478
Wes Hart: How'd it go?

431
00:23:02,936 --> 00:23:07,830
Jonathon: Monday was great. Tuesday was
fine. Wednesday, Theo flooded the bathroom.

432
00:23:08,190 --> 00:23:08,794
Wes Hart: How?

433
00:23:09,190 --> 00:23:12,780
Jonathon: I genuinely do not know.
I turned around to get a towel.

434
00:23:12,790 --> 00:23:16,430
I turned back and there was water
going under the door into the hallway.

435
00:23:16,490 --> 00:23:20,050
He was just sitting in the
tub smiling at me. Very proud.

436
00:23:20,407 --> 00:23:24,420
Jonathon: And I'll be honest with you, there
